thunder: CCCI AT port ttyC0 as a real TTY, for ModemManager.

/dev/ttyC0 (CCCI_UART2, the modem AT channel) is a CCCI char port: open,
read, write and poll only. tcsetattr() on it fails with ENOTTY, so
ModemManager (and any serial tool) cannot set the port up and never probes
the modem.

Add a TTY port type to eccci (port/port_tty.c) and switch only the ttyC0
entry of port_cfg.c to it. The CCCI side is the char port's: same
rx_skb_list and rx_length_th, the kernel adds and strips the ccci_header
(userspace still sees raw AT text), CCCI_UART2_RX data is queued while the
port is closed, and the first open/last close call port_user_register()/
port_user_unregister(), so a holder of ttyC0 is still CRIT_USR_MUXD and
blocks or permits an MD start exactly as before. In front of it sits a
one-line tty_driver named after the port ("ttyC" + name_base 0), so the
node stays /dev/ttyC0; the char ports ttyC1/C2/C3/C5/C6 keep their
ccci_node devices. The tty's parent is the eccci platform device, so udev
can match KERNEL=="ttyC0" and tag it for ModemManager.

RX: recv_skb only queues (port_recv_skb) and kicks a work item that feeds
the flip buffer; while the tty is throttled or the flip buffer is full the
data stays queued and, past rx_length_th, the CCIF queue keeps it in shared
memory (the back pressure of a char reader; nothing is dropped). TX: split
to CCCI_MTU, pool skb and HIF send without blocking; a full ring returns a
short count and a delayed work wakes the writer. Writes need MD READY
(-ENODEV otherwise). Termios starts raw 8N1 B115200 with CLOCAL and is
accepted and ignored; TIOCMGET reports a connected line.

